-- title:
--   Finite-dimensionality of Riemann–Roch spaces on X₀(q)_{ℚ̄}
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime number and let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding `coeffEmb`, of `modularFunctionFieldFull (1 * q)`, the latter being the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb{Q})$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the family `divisorExpansions (1 * q)` (the level is written $1 \cdot q$, not $q$). Let $D$ be a divisor of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, that is, a finitely supported function from the places of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $\mathbb{Z}$, a place being a valuation subring of $F$ that contains the image of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring. The assertion is that the associated Riemann–Roch space, namely the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-submodule of those $f \in F$ with $v(f) \le \exp(D\,v)$ in $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$ for every place $v$, where $v$ is the adic valuation attached to $v$, is a finite-dimensional $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-vector space.
--
--   This is the finiteness half of the Riemann–Roch theory for the function field of the modular curve $X_0(q)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$: the spaces $L(D)$ are finite-dimensional, so that dimension arguments may be run on them. It is used by the constructions producing functions in $L(D)$ with prescribed residues or prescribed order at a place, where finite-dimensionality supplies the required linear-algebra input.
